          "type": "turn",
          "question": "type 1 here, been sick all day, meter says 24 and the ketone strip went dark purple, my breath smells odd",
          "answer": "The symptoms you described involve your **metabolic system** (high blood sugar and ketones) and potentially your **respiratory system** (odd-smelling breath, which can indicate changes in metabolism affecting breath).\n\nIn the context of type 1 diabetes, these symptoms strongly suggest **diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)**. This is a serious complication that occurs when your body does not have enough insulin to allow blood sugar into your cells for energy. Instead, your liver breaks down fat for fuel, a process that produces acids called ketones. These ketones can build up to dangerous levels in your blood.\n\nWhen a clinician assesses these symptoms, they would be looking to confirm DKA and identify its cause. Common triggers for DKA include:\n*   **Illness or infection:** As you mentioned being sick, this is a significant factor. Infections can cause the body to produce more hormones that counteract insulin, leading to higher blood sugar and ketone production (Diabetic Ketoacidosis, National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases).\n*   **Missed insulin doses:** Not taking enough insulin or missing doses can lead to DKA.\n*   **Problems with insulin pump:** Malfunctions in insulin pumps can disrupt insulin delivery.\n\nThe dark purple ketone strip indicates a very high level of ketones, and a blood sugar reading of 24 (mmol/L) is significantly elevated. The \"odd-smelling breath\" is often described as fruity or like nail polish remover, caused by the body trying to get rid of excess ketones through breathing.\n\nA clinician would assess your hydration status, electrolyte balance, and kidney function, in addition to confirming blood sugar and ketone levels. They would also look for any underlying infection.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
